# DIS build context exclusions.
#
# THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING FOR SECRETS. docker-compose builds DIS with
# `context: ./dis_backend`, so the repo-root .dockerignore does NOT apply here —
# a root-level rule protects the CAS images and silently misses this one.
#
# Without it, `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile baked dis_backend/.env straight into an
# image layer. Verified 2026-08-14 against the built image: /app/.env was present
# and readable, carrying JWT_SECRET, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID,
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, AWS_SESSION_TOKEN, DIS_SERVICE_TOKEN and
# DIS_BEDROCK_*. Anyone able to pull or `docker save` that image had the
# credentials, and deleting the file in a later layer would NOT remove it — the
# layer keeps it. The file is gitignored, so this was purely a packaging leak.
#
# DIS receives its configuration at runtime through compose's
# `env_file: ./dis_backend/.env`, so excluding it from the build changes nothing
# about how the service runs.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Secrets and credential material of any other shape.
*.pem
*.key
credentials

# Tests are source (they live in git) but nothing in the image runs them: the
# Dockerfile never invokes pytest and the production stage does not install it.
# ~800KB of dead weight and needless read surface in a running container.
tests/

__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
*.db
*.sqlite3
venv/
.venv/
env/
logs/
*.log
data/
uploads/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# WSL/NTFS alternate-data-stream artifacts — 37 of these sit beside real files in
# this tree. Untracked, meaningless to Linux, and pure build-context weight.
*:Zone.Identifier
